# size :: tree -> snat (count of nodes)
# _s a b c ⟶ a c (b c)
_s = \a \b \c a c $ b c
# _triage self:  △ ⟶ id;  △ u ⟶ self u;  △ u v ⟶ compose (self v) (self u)
_triage = \self triage id self $ \u
      _s
        (\v compose (self v))
        (\v self u)
# _functional_smallest self t n ⟶ △ (_triage self t n)
_functional_smallest = \self \x compose Snat.succ $ _triage self x
size__eager_125 = \x (fix__wait _functional_smallest) x Snat.zero

# Pinned to fix__wait, the knot it was searched against — core's `fix` is now
# the quine knot below, which would silently shrink it to 118 and invalidate the
# count recorded here. It held the record when it was found, and the search that
# established it is why the two programs below leave its step alone and change
# only the knot around it: it is minimal at 125 nodes among fix__wait-knotted
# forms — seed wrapper (5) + fix__wait knot (44: a 17-node unfolder, paid twice)
# + body (76) — by exhaustive enumeration (~150M candidates: all knot unfolders
# to 16 nodes, wrapper/dispatch shells to 14, fork-case replacements to enum-15
# with combinator atoms, every normal-form subtree against all trees up to 11
# nodes) plus ~60 reformulations through the compiler, best alternative 129.
# What that enumeration did turn up was five independent same-size substitution
# sites, so the tree above is one of 31 sibling 125-node normal forms with
# nothing to choose between them — and the 118 below dominates all 31 outright,
# fewer nodes and fewer reductions both (src/snat/bench.lamb).

# Smaller and still eager: a knot that costs 37 nodes where fix__wait's costs 44.
# Where fix__wait keeps re-entry delayed behind wait — a 17-node unfolder,
# paid twice — the knot below is a quine: the loop rebuilds itself from the
# copy it carries, and stays eager-safe because the rebuild only ever
# happens under the input constructor just consumed, one unfolding per
# node. Every intermediate term is a value; nothing here waits. The step is
# _functional_smallest, unchanged: only the knot around it is new, and the
# certificates below are the same machine-checked ones the 125 above
# carries. That knot generalizes, and is now core's `fix` — so
# size__eager_118 is exactly \x (fix _functional_smallest) x Snat.zero. It
# stays spelled out here because size__eager_103 below builds on _k too.
# The knot is also as tight as enumeration reaches, assuming nothing about
# its shape: read the whole program as \x (△ P (△ (compose step R))) x △,
# with P = △ _k and R = △ P the twice-paid pieces. Every tree to fourteen
# nodes fails in P's seat — forks included, not just stems over a quine
# core, and every core to thirteen inside _k fails too, for this step and
# the 103's both — and every tree to fifteen fails in R's: of the 180340
# candidates none so much as rebuilds △ P (△ x), so rule (2)'s duplication
# cannot construct the carried copy cheaper than carrying it. The same kind
# of enumeration that established the 125's minimality, pointed at the knot.
# _selfstem q ⟶ q (△ q)
_selfstem = \q q (△ q)
# _k z ⟶ triage △ △ _selfstem — fourteen nodes, paid twice, plus nine of
# wiring — so on the packed fork rule (2) builds:  _k z (△ q z) ⟶ q (△ q) z
_k = const (triage △ △ _selfstem)
# ties self := △ (△ _k) (△ _code):
#   self z ⟶ _k z (△ _code z) ⟶ _code (△ _code) z ⟶ _functional_smallest self z
_code = \sq _functional_smallest (△ (△ _k) sq)
size__eager_118 = \x (△ (△ _k) (△ _code)) x △

# The same knot around a different step reaches 103. The record step
# composes the two child counts; this one owns no composition machinery at
# all: a fork's left child is counted by stacking its nodes onto the right
# child — self u v reduces to △ⁿ v for n = |u| — and recursing on that
# chain, which is one node smaller than △ u v was. Its fork case is two
# bare applications of self where the record step pays compose twice and a
# shared S to spell them.
# Found by annealing over whole trees with an eager evaluator as the
# filter — the same deletion-and-replacement spirit as #20, pointed at the
# eager family — first as a 110 tied with fix__wait's knot, then reformulated
# through the compiler and retied with _k. Every unfolding consumes one
# node of state, so reduction terminates in every order — but recursion on
# a rebuilt chain is not recursion on a subtree, which no structural FOLD
# closes. The eager certifier carries size-measure rules for exactly this
# shape (RGEN and GEN-TREE, src/certify/size): the chain becomes a
# minted tree variable of known size, strictly smaller than the split
# variable, and the induction closes — so the certificate below is as
# machine-checked as the record's. The lazy certifier has no such rules,
# and its false stays the conservative false the header describes. Of the
# weakly normalizing records further down, the 78, the 95 and the 100
# undercut the eager ones.
# _chain_step self t n ⟶ △ (case t of  △ ⟶ n;  △ u ⟶ self u n;  △ u v ⟶ self (self u v) n)
_chain_step = \self \x compose Snat.succ $ triage id self (\u \v self (self u v)) x
# ties self := △ (△ _k) (△ _chain_code):  self z ⟶ _chain_step self z
_chain_code = \sq _chain_step (△ (△ _k) sq)
size__eager_103 = \x (△ (△ _k) (△ _chain_code)) x △

# The record, and the smallest of these this repository can run under its own
# rule — so it is what __smallest names. (size__smallest__wn further down is
# the normal-order record, at 78 nodes.)
# Smallest is not cheapest: the chain it recurses on is rebuilt rather than
# taken apart, and it pays five to six times `size`'s reductions for it, so it
# is the record and not the default — see src/snat/bench.lamb.
size__smallest = size__eager_103

_to_string = Snat.to_string
size__eager_118 (△ (△ △) (△ △))
# = 5
size__eager_103 (△ (△ △) (△ △))
# = 5
size__eager_118 size__eager_103
# = 103
size__eager_103 size__eager_118
# = 118
size__eager_103 size__eager_103
# = 103

_to_string = Bool.to_source
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__eager_118
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size size__eager_118
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__eager_103
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size size__eager_103
# = false

# Smaller still, once the program no longer has to terminate under eager
# evaluation. Nathan Farlow found all of these by search: the six older ones
# by deleting subtrees of a known program — sometimes several at once — and
# looking for smaller replacements (lambada-llc/tree-calculus#20); then the 95
# from a 103 by replacing single subtrees, and the 78 from the 95 by replacing
# two at a time, candidates enumerated on a GPU some 270 times faster than the
# CPU searches behind #20 (#23). Every one of them hangs an eager evaluator,
# which is what __wn marks — weakly normalizing: the normal form exists, but
# eager reduction never reaches it — so they are applied through
# Reflect.lazy_eval. They are named by node count rather than by rank, so
# that finding a smaller one adds a line instead of renumbering the rest.
#
# Search results are not obliged to decompose, but these do. Written as trees
# the eight are walls of triangles; taken apart they are two builds of one
# idea — a recursive loop tied without any delay, entered through a small
# dispatch, on an input packed into a fork — the older six differing in the
# step the loop drives and in how the input reaches it, the record pair in
# how little of everything else is left. The parts below rebuild each record
# tree node for node: every definition in this section terminates eagerly and
# reduces to exactly the tree the search found, so the counts at the bottom
# of this file are still the search's counts. Only running them needs normal
# order.
#
# First the tree all eight lean on. Where fix__wait above keeps its recursion
# delayed behind wait — the 44 nodes size__eager_125 pays, its unfolder twice
# included — this section's delay budget is zero.
# _fork_self f ⟶ △ f (△ f) (△ f);  on a fork:  _fork_self (△ u v) ⟶ v (△ u v)
_fork_self = \f △ f (△ f) (△ f)
# △ (△ (△ (△ △) △)) △, seven nodes: a fork handed to its own right branch,
# nothing delaying the re-application — which is exactly why nothing built
# on it terminates eagerly. The six older programs tie their loops with it;
# the record pair spends it on the door instead.

# The record pair, the 78 and the 95, came in one report (#23): one machine,
# two steps, and the machine is this section's own parts reassembled.
# _dup w ⟶ △ w (△ △ w) (△ △ w);  on a fork:  _dup (△ u v) ⟶ △ u v (△ u v)
_dup = \w △ w (△ △ w) (△ △ w)
# △ (△ (△ (△ △) (△ △))) (△ △), nine nodes: self-application, bare. Set it
# beside a step as  code := _s (const step) _dup,  and the loop ties itself:
#   code z ⟶ step (_dup z),   so   self := _dup code ⟶ code code ⟶ step self.
# Re-entry is the nine nodes above, paid once. The delay fix__wait buys with
# 44 nodes and fix with 37 — one unfolding per input constructor consumed —
# _dup simply does not buy, and an eager evaluator handed any input unfolds
# self without end.
# _pack step t ⟶ △ code t
_pack = \step △ (_s (const step) _dup)
# The stem over code: code and input side by side, waiting to be spilled.
# _dispatch (△ code t) k ⟶ _fork_self code t k ⟶ _dup code t k ⟶ step self t k
_dispatch = triage △ △ _fork_self
# The door, the six's knot re-hired: its fork case hands code to _fork_self,
# whose right branch is _dup, and the input follows self into the loop. (No
# input reaches the leaf and stem cases; △ and △ are the cheapest trees to
# hold the seats.)
# _wn_size step t ⟶ step self t k,  where k = const △ t reduces to △ only
# when the step forces it
_wn_size = \step \t _dispatch (_pack step t) (const △ t)
# The machine: thirty-four nodes of harness around whatever step arrives,
# where size__eager_103 pays 42 and size__eager_125 pays 49.
# _flat △ ⟶ △;   _flat (△ m) ⟶ △ m;   _flat (△ m n) ⟶ △ m
_flat = triage △ △ (\m \n △ m)
# Identity on a count arriving, prune on anything else. Eleven nodes whose
# work shows up only in the 78.
# _step_95 self t k:  △ ⟶ △ k;  △ u ⟶ △ (self u k);  △ u v ⟶ △ (self (self u v) k)
# giving  self w k ⟶ △ⁿ k,  n = |w|,  for every k whatsoever
_step_95 = \self \t \k Snat.succ $ triage id self (compose (compose self) self) t k
# Each case emits its own node's succ; a fork counts its left child onto its
# right — self u v is △^|u| v — and recurses on that chain, one node shorter
# than the fork it replaces. Not merely _chain_step's 61 nodes but its very
# tree: the two spellings compile to one term, checked below, so the whole
# 103-against-95 difference is the knot.
size__wn_95 = _wn_size _step_95
# _step_78 self t k:  △ ⟶ _flat k k;  △ u ⟶ _flat k (self u k);
#                     △ u v ⟶ _flat k (self u self v k)
_step_78 = \self \t \k _flat k $ triage id self (_s self (const self)) t k
# Seventeen nodes carved out of the 95's step by handing the loop itself in
# as the accumulator: k is only ever △ — where _flat k is Snat.succ — or
# self. On self the pruning closes a small algebra, SA := △ (△ △ (△ (△ _flat))):
#   whnf self        =  △ SA …            (… the live half, pruned away below)
#   _flat self       ⟶  △ SA
#   △ SA X v         ⟶  △ (△ _flat) (X v)
#   △ (△ _flat) Y k  ⟶  _flat k (Y k)
# so the fork case's self u self stacks |u| such layers, each spending one
# _flat k — one succ — as the rest of the input and finally the zero arrive:
# self u self v △ ⟶ △^(|u|+|v|) △. The 95's law holds for every k, the 78's
# only for its own loop's two, and those seventeen nodes are the price of
# the difference.
size__wn_78 = _wn_size _step_78
size__smallest__wn = size__wn_78

# The trees, read back out and held against the strings the search reported
# (#23):
_to_string = String.to_source
Serialize.ternary_encode size__wn_78
# = "212120220021210001212021201120112200212010021212001211002121200010212101010200"
Serialize.ternary_encode size__wn_95
# = "21212022002121000121202120112011200212120012110021212021202120001021202120001021100212101010200"

# The step identity, checked: the 95 drives the eager record's step, tree
# for tree.
_to_string = Bool.to_source
equal _step_95 _chain_step
# = true

# That identity is also why delay does not squeeze an eager record out of
# the pair, and the failure has been mapped seat by seat. Wrap any single
# subtree of either record in wait — all 173 positions — and the 78 absorbs
# it nowhere: most wraps leave the self-application bare and diverge, and
# the rest sit where the machine reads trees as data, so the wrapped tree
# is different data and the count comes out wrong. The 95 absorbs it in
# exactly one seat, _dup's own, and what that buys is worth keeping:
# size__eager_111 t ⟶ _step_95 self' t (const △ t),  a value
#   self' := wait _dup code',  self' z ⟶ _dup code' z ⟶ _step_95 self' z
size__eager_111 = \t _dispatch (△ (_s (const _step_95) (wait _dup)) t) (const △ t)
# The weak record made eager-legal: the same machine, wait _dup seated
# where _dup sat, nothing else changed — the divergent application is now a
# value, and each unfolding waits for a constructor of the input. Sixteen
# nodes of wait around the nine of _dup make 111 — eight over the record
# from the other side, since retying the same step with _k is
# size__eager_103 itself, node for node. Nor is there a leaner knot for the
# seat: every tree to sixteen nodes fails in it, under this step and the
# 78's both — 490912 candidates — so eager legality by this route starts at
# the record's own 103, if a seventeen-node knot exists at all, and wait's
# twenty-five is the only one known. The 78's step survives no knot but its
# own: with _k seated it assembles an 86-node program that miscounts —
# _flat reads a whnf shape only _dup has — and no tree of thirteen nodes or
# fewer repairs _flat's seat.
_to_string = Snat.to_string
size__eager_111 (△ (△ △) (△ △))
# = 5
_to_string = Bool.to_source
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__eager_111
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size size__eager_111
# = false

# The six the pair supersedes, three architectures tied with _fork_self.
# _loop step z ⟶ step (_fork_self z);  writing c := _loop step:
#   X := c (const c) ⟶ step X
_loop = \step _s (const step) _fork_self
# _loop step = △ (△ (△ △ step)) _fork_self — the pair's code with _fork_self
# in _dup's seat: a fixpoint that unfolds once per demand in normal order,
# and without end under eager evaluation.
# _tie c ⟶ c (const c)
_tie = \c c (const c)
# _tie c is that X. The programs never reduce it while being built: _tie and
# the loop sit side by side as values, and only the input's arrival at run
# time applies one to the other.
# _tie_open c ⟶ △ (const c) (_tie c);  _tie_open c (△ a b) ⟶ _tie c a b
_tie_open = \c _s △ c (const c)
# A fork, so a packed input spills both halves into the knot. (Its leaf and
# stem cases are △ and c; no input below ever reaches them.)
# _lagged_size tie loop t ⟶ tie loop (△ △ t)
_lagged_size = \tie \loop \t const tie t loop (const t)
# Four of the six share this skeleton: enter the loop through tie, handing
# it the input dragged one constructor behind. The states the loops then
# chew on are built the same way: counts in progress, stems over a fork
# lagging what is left of the input. Their workhorse:
# _b2 w ⟶ △ (△ (△ △ w))  — count two, then the lag — = compose w, the same tree
_b2 = △ (△ (△ △ △)) (△ (△ (△ △ △)) (△ △))
# One tree being both "add two" and "compose" is why it was one of the
# 125's same-size substitution sites, and why the searches below keep
# rediscovering it.

# The 100 and the 104 are one architecture. Their step triages the state — a
# leaf answers △, a stem answers itself — and a fork spills into _park and
# _lag2, one half swallowed, the count redone.
# _park w ⟶ triage w (△ w) (compose Snat.succ (△ w))
_park = \w △ (△ w (△ w)) (compose Snat.succ (△ w))
# w parked as the answer for a leaf, grafted under whatever arrives instead.
# _lag2 w ⟶ △ (△ (△ △ (_b2 w)))  — _b2 after _b2, spelled as one chain
_lag2 = \w △ (△ (const (_b2 w)))
# _step_100 loop ⟶ triage △ △ (compose (_b2 loop) _park)
_step_100 = \loop triage △ △ (_lag2 loop _park)
# _loop_counted step z ⟶ step (compose Snat.succ (_fork_self z))
_loop_counted = \step _s (const step) (compose (compose Snat.succ) _fork_self)
# _loop with one count folded into the knot itself: each unfolding's stem
# already emitted.
size__wn_100 = _lagged_size _tie (_loop_counted _step_100)
# _step_104 loop ⟶ triage △ △ (compose (compose Snat.succ) (compose (_b2 loop) _park))
_step_104 = \loop triage △ △ (compose (compose Snat.succ) (_lag2 loop _park))
# The same step emitting that count itself, which costs the four nodes the
# 100 saves.
size__wn_104 = _lagged_size _tie (_loop _step_104)

# The 103 and the 106 share their count ladder — and nothing else:
# _recount w ⟶ △ (△ (compose Snat.succ (compose Snat.succ Snat.succ w)))
_recount = \w △ (△ (compose Snat.succ (compose Snat.succ Snat.succ w)))
# Two counts, then one more composed over w pushed two counts further on.
# _state_103 w ⟶ △ (△ (_s _recount (const w)))
_state_103 = \w △ (△ (_s _recount (const w)))
# _step_103 loop ⟶ _state_103 loop (loop △)
_step_103 = \loop _state_103 loop (loop △)
# So X ⟶ _state_103 X (X △): the state rebuilt around the loop, applied to
# the loop restarted at a bare leaf.
# _tie_lagged c ⟶ △ K (c K),  K ⟶ const (compose _b2 c)
_tie_lagged = \c _s △ c (const (compose _b2 c))
# _tie_open, except the knot closes over K: the 103's loop never sees
# itself bare — every re-entry reduces to _b2 X.
size__wn_103 = _lagged_size _tie_lagged (_loop _step_103)
# _state_106 w ⟶ △ (△ (_s _recount (const (_b2 w))))
_state_106 = \w △ (△ (_s _recount (const (_b2 w))))
# _step_106 loop ⟶ _state_106 loop (_b2 loop △)
_step_106 = \loop _state_106 loop (_b2 loop △)
# As the 103 with both slots pushed through _b2, and _b2 X △ is X composed
# after △.
size__wn_106 = _lagged_size _tie_open (_loop _step_106)

# The 108 and the 113 drive one step, and it is not theirs: it is, node for
# node, the 76-node body size__eager_125's fix__wait knot drives — the expect test
# below reads it back out of size__eager_125 — with everything around it
# re-carved. That is the sense in which #20's deletions started from the
# eager program.
# _b2__left w ⟶ compose Snat.succ Snat.succ (const w)
_b2__left = \w compose Snat.succ Snat.succ (const w)
# _b2 once more — count two, then lag — the same function and the same 12
# nodes with the composition leaning the other way, so a different tree.
_carved_states = \w _b2__left (compose Snat.succ Snat.succ (compose _b2__left w))
# _carved z:  △ ⟶ △ Snat.two △;  △ w ⟶ z w;  △ u v ⟶ _carved_states z (compose const z) u v
_carved = \z △ (triage Snat.two △ z) (_carved_states z (compose const z))
# A leaf answers, a stem re-enters the loop, a fork falls to the states.
# _step_carved loop ⟶ compose (compose Snat.succ) (_carved loop)
_step_carved = \loop compose (compose Snat.succ) (_carved loop)
# One more count composed onto everything the machinery underneath returns.
# size__wn_108 t ⟶ _tie_open (_loop _step_carved) (△ t △) ⟶ X t △
size__wn_108 = \t const _tie_open t (_loop _step_carved) (△ t △)
# The skeleton again, but the input packed the other way around — △ t △.
# size__wn_113 t ⟶ _tie (_loop _step_carved) t △ — the same X t △ call
size__wn_113 = \t _s _tie (const t) (_loop _step_carved) △
# No dispatch at all. The assembled tree is size__eager_125's own, with one
# cut: its 19-node delayed re-entry replaced by the 7-node _fork_self.

# The carved-from claim, checked: the step the 108 and the 113 loop over sits
# whole inside size__eager_125 — left, left, right, right, left, left, right
# from the top.
# _left:  △ ⟶ △;  △ u ⟶ u;  △ u v ⟶ u        _right:  △ u v ⟶ v
_left = triage △ id const
_right = triage △ id (const id)
_to_string = Bool.to_source
equal _step_carved (_right (_left (_left (_right (_right (_left (_left size__eager_125)))))))
# = true

# _size_body self t n ⟶ △ (case t of  △ ⟶ n;  △ u ⟶ self self u n;
#                                     △ u v ⟶ self self v (self self u n))
_size_body = \self \x compose △ $ triage (\self id) (\u \self self self u) (\v \u \self compose (self self u) (self self v)) x self
# size__fastest t ⟶ _size_body _size_body t △
size__fastest = \x (self_apply _size_body) x △
# size t ⟶ the same, the self-application held behind wait until t arrives
size = \x (wait self_apply _size_body) x △

# Certify.Size.computes_size proves a program returns its argument's node count for
# every tree, by reading it as the tree it is; src/certify/size has the how.
_to_string = Bool.to_source
Certify.Size.computes_size size
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size size__fastest
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size size__eager_125
# = true

# A true stands on a proof, and the proof is an induction tree that can be
# looked at: Certify.Size.certify_size returns it, Certify.Size.render draws it. For
# the 125, whose plain subtree recursion makes it the smallest proof here —
# SPLIT with a case per shape of the split variable, PEEL per emitted
# successor, GEN naming a subterm's count, FOLD closing an instance of an
# induction hypothesis.
_to_string = Option.match "no proof" Certify.Size.render
Certify.Size.certify_size size__eager_125
# = SPLIT t
#     PEEL
#       ZERO
#     GEN g1
#       PEEL
#         VALUE g1
#     GEN g2
#       CUT [g2]
#         PEEL
#           SPLIT t1
#             PEEL
#               END
#             PEEL
#               FOLD
#             CUT [|t10|,*k1]
#               PEEL
#                 FOLD*
#         VALUE g2
_to_string = Bool.to_source

# Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly proves that and more: that reduction terminates
# in every order, so these are `size` for an evaluator that reduces arguments
# before it uses them, which is what this repository's rule asks of them. The
# five above are written to that rule and pass.
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__fastest
# = true
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__eager_125
# = true

# And it is what the __wn ones fail, on the very first state: reducing them
# eagerly under a variable input spends the whole budget without reaching a
# normal form, which is the same wall an eager evaluator hits on a real one.
# That is what separates the two families here, read off the programs rather
# than taken on faith.
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_78
# = false
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_95
# = false
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_100
# = false
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_103
# = false
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_104
# = false
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_106
# = false
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_108
# = false
Certify.Size.computes_size_eagerly size__wn_113
# = false

# Certify.Size.computes_size asks only for the count, in normal order, and its
# extended rule system (src/certify/size) closes over two of them: the
# induction abstracts away the input copies their states drag one constructor
# behind the live one, and a tail atom caps the spec their left-nested forks
# would otherwise grow without bound. A true is a proof that the program
# returns its argument's node count on every tree — which is more than any
# run of it below can say.
Certify.Size.computes_size size__wn_78
# = false
